00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:33.479
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): When we talk about digital identity, we often think of using a single cryptographic key, one private key used for a digital signature or to authenticate. It's simple and efficient, but it also creates a single point of failure and a significant potential risk.

46
00:04:33.519 --> 00:04:49.149
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Multi-signature and threshold keys are designed to solve that problem. Instead of depending on one private key, control is divided among several. To authorize an action, sign a document, or authenticate, more than one key has to participate.

47
00:04:49.149 --> 00:05:00.739
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In a full multi-signature setup, every key must sign before the operation is approved. In a threshold scheme, only a subset needs to, maybe 2 out of 3, or 3 out of 5.

48
00:05:00.739 --> 00:05:18.779
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Think of it like a secure vault that can only be opened when several people turn their keys at the same time. This idea is also used with decentralized identifiers, or DIDs. A DID documents verification method doesn't have to contain just one public key, it can define more complex arrangements.

49
00:05:18.779 --> 00:05:37.689
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): For instance, it might require that 2 out of 3 keys sign before a credential is issued, or that both your personal key and a backup key held by your organization approve before control is proven. The benefits are significant. A single stolen key is no longer enough to impersonate you.

50
00:05:37.689 --> 00:05:43.749
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Responsibility can be shared across an organization without giving any one person full control.

51
00:05:43.749 --> 00:06:04.939
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): And you gain flexibility. You can define security policies that fit your risk level. Maybe all keys must agree for critical actions, but only a majority for routine operations. Of course, distributing control adds complexity. Multiple signers have to coordinate, and systems need to be able to collect and verify several keys.

52
00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:20.159
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): But in high-stakes environments, like finance, governance, or enterprise identity management, that added effort pays off in resilience. With multi-signature and threshold keys, control isn't concentrated in a single key, it's distributed.

53
00:06:20.159 --> 00:06:26.789
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Enabling identities that are not only verifiable, but also stronger, safer, and more resistant to compromise.

