186
00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:21.160
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Okay, so on to key pre-rotation… Which is… Back here…

187
00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:43.530
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Keys and digital identities come in pairs, a private key and a public key. You keep the private key secret, usually stored in a secure digital wallet. The public key, on the other hand, is meant to be shared, for example, by publishing it in a decentralized identifier, or DID, document, so that others can use it to verify you.

188
00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:51.049
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With these keys, you can do powerful things. You can authenticate yourself online and log into services without passwords.

189
00:16:51.050 --> 00:17:01.439
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): You can also digitally sign documents, such as a university signing degrees for its graduates, and anyone can check that signature against the public key. But there is a catch.

190
00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:11.569
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): If your private key is ever compromised, maybe your wallet gets hacked, or the storage system is tricked, your identity could be at risk, meaning that someone could impersonate you online.

191
00:17:12.089 --> 00:17:18.359
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That's why it's good practice to rotate your keys frequently, retire the old one, and start using a new one.

192
00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:33.130
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In DID systems, these updates may be reported in the DID document data, so that anyone resolving the DID knows which key is current. Still, that raises a problem. What if an attacker steals your key and performs the rotation before you can?

193
00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:41.009
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Suddenly, they look like the rightful controller of your did, and you can't reclaim control because the system now trusts their key.

194
00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:54.819
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): This is where key pre-rotation comes in. With pre-rotation, every time you create a public-private key pair, you also generate the next pair in advance, making sure to keep them secure and available for future use.

195
00:17:54.820 --> 00:18:04.319
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Instead of publishing the full future public key, you publish a cryptographic digest of that public key, a commitment to the key pair you'll use next.

196
00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:20.250
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Later, when you rotate, you reveal the actual public key, and anyone can check that it matches the digest you committed to earlier. Where this pre-rotation data is stored is specific to each DID method, and is not part of the DID core specification.

197
00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:27.289
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Some did methods, like DidWebS and DIDWebVH, support key pre-rotation, and other methods do not.

198
00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:45.959
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With key pre-rotation, even if someone compromises your current private key, they can't hijack your DID, because they don't know the next key you've already committed to. Where supported, pre-rotation makes key rotation predictable and tamper-resistant, securing DIDs against takeover.

