223
00:20:49.920 --> 00:21:07.489
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): When you resolve a decentralized identifier, or DID, you don't just get back keys, you also can get something called service endpoints. A service endpoint is like the address book entry for your DID. It tells others where to reach you, what protocols you support, or which services you're connected to.

224
00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:24.420
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): For example, a DID document might list an endpoint for secure messaging with DIDCOM, another for a credential exchange, or even a simple URL for a profile page. Each service endpoint is described with a type, an ID, and a URI. But here's an important point.

225
00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:40.430
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): DIDs themselves don't handle routing. They don't deliver your messages. Instead, service endpoints connect your DID world to existing infrastructure, like the internet, peer-to-peer networks, or even email. That's how messages actually get from point A to point B.

226
00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:50.379
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): So think of service endpoints as the bridge. The keys in your DID prove who you are, but the service endpoints tell others how to find you and which communication channels to use.

